GLdc 1.1.1: five bugs. Two in mipmap generation, one in glBindTexture, two in
vertex colour and alpha.

GL/framebuffer.c

 1. _glCalculateAverageTexel() read the texture format from the wrong bits.
    The format is a three-bit field at bits 27-29 of the PVR format word;
    bit 26 is GPU_TXRFMT_TWIDDLED. The old expression folded the twiddle flag
    in as the format's low bit, so twiddled ARGB4444 -- what
    _glGenerateMipmapTwiddled() produces -- was averaged as ARGB1555.

 2. The ARGB4444 channel readers returned the raw 0-15 nibble, but
    PACK_ARGB4444 is a 0-255 -> 0-15 packer (_PACK4(v) = v * 0xF / 0xFF).
    For any n <= 16, n * 15 <= 240 < 255, so the division truncated every
    channel to zero and every ARGB4444 mip texel came out 0x0000. Fixed in
    the readers, not the macro: PACK_ARGB4444's other caller is the palette
    path in texture.c, which passes 0-255 components.

    These two interact. Fixing only (1) routes twiddled ARGB4444 into the
    4444 branch for the first time, where (2) then zeroes it -- so (1) alone
    turns wrong colours into transparent black. Both are needed.

GL/texture.c

 3. glBindTexture()'s recovery path shadowed its own variable:

        TextureObject* txr = named_array_get(...);
        if(!txr) {
            TextureObject* txr = named_array_reserve(...);   /* shadows */
            _glInitializeTextureObject(txr, texture);
        }
        TEXTURE_UNITS[ACTIVE_TEXTURE] = txr;                 /* still NULL */
        gl_assert(TEXTURE_UNITS[ACTIVE_TEXTURE]->index == texture);

    so the branch initialised an object and threw the pointer away, leaving
    the outer txr NULL for the store and the assertion below it. Binding an
    id that did not come from glGenTextures -- or a reserve that failed
    because the heap was exhausted -- asserted inside the driver instead of
    returning an error. Observed as a dark screen with no audio and no
    message, after eighteen failed sound allocations:

      Out of memory. Requested sbrk_base 8d009000, was 8cfd4000, diff 217088
      GL ERROR: GL_INVALID_VALUE when calling _glTexImage2DValidate
      Assertion failed at GL/texture.c:751

GL/attributes.c

 4. glDrawArrays with no colour array enabled filled every vertex white.
    calcReadDiffuseFunc() returned _fillWhiteARGB for the disabled case, so
    the generic path ignored glColor* entirely; only the fast path (3-float
    positions, 2-float UVs) and immediate mode read _glCurrentColor(). Aleph
    One's sprites and walls use GL_DOUBLE positions and its fader quad uses
    2-float positions, so none of them hit the fast path: sprites and unsplit
    walls drew unlit, and every screen fade -- red for damage, green for a
    pickup -- drew as an opaque white quad because the tint colour and its
    alpha never reached the vertices. The disabled case now fills from the
    current colour, which is what the spec says and what the other two paths
    already did.

GL/platform.h

 5. Untextured polygons could not blend. CompilePolyHeader() simulates "no
    texture" with an 8x8 opaque white texture, and set the texture
    environment to GPU_TXRENV_MODULATE -- the PowerVR mode whose output
    alpha is the *texture's* alpha, so vertex alpha was discarded and every
    untextured translucent quad drew opaque. Aleph One's screen fades and the
    pause scrim are exactly that. MODULATEALPHA multiplies texture alpha
    (1.0) by vertex alpha; it is what GL_MODULATE already maps to for real
    textures (texture.c).

Apply to both the dist/ and build/ copies of the libGL kos-port, then rebuild:
make GL in build/libGL-1.1.1, and copy the resulting libGL.a over
inst/lib/libGL.a. See README.DC.md, 'Patched toolchain'.

